1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,148
You've made six real decisions across this section: whether the problem is even a

2
00:00:05,148 --> 00:00:09,827
missing-fact problem, what stages the pipeline needs, how to chunk each content

3
00:00:09,827 --> 00:00:14,624
type, what retrieval quality actually means, which production failures apply, and

4
00:00:14,624 --> 00:00:17,112
how permissions and freshness get handled.

5
00:00:17,112 --> 00:00:19,540
Here's where all six become one document.

6
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:21,791
This is A06, attached to this lecture.

7
00:00:21,791 --> 00:00:26,707
Open it now and fill sections in as we walk through them, the same way you did with

8
00:00:26,707 --> 00:00:28,721
the PRD template in Section three.

9
00:00:28,721 --> 00:00:32,689
Think of this document as sitting inside the PRD, not replacing it.

10
00:00:32,689 --> 00:00:37,486
The acceptance criteria section from Section three still names the threshold your

11
00:00:37,486 --> 00:00:38,730
feature has to clear.

12
00:00:38,730 --> 00:00:43,350
This design doc is what makes that threshold achievable in the first place, by

13
00:00:43,350 --> 00:00:46,608
specifying the retrieval system underneath it properly.

14
00:00:46,608 --> 00:00:48,148
One document sets the bar.

15
00:00:48,148 --> 00:00:51,346
The other explains how the system actually reaches it.

16
00:00:51,896 --> 00:00:56,065
Read the first row before any of the others, because it's the one that decides

17
00:00:56,065 --> 00:00:58,951
whether the rest of this document should exist at all.

18
00:00:58,951 --> 00:01:02,852
Section two's decision tree already gave you the test: is this actually a

19
00:01:02,852 --> 00:01:03,974
missing-fact problem.

20
00:01:03,974 --> 00:01:06,860
If a design doc can't answer that cleanly, stop there.

21
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:11,136
The other five sections are wasted effort on a system solving the wrong problem.

22
00:01:11,136 --> 00:01:15,518
Past that gate, the remaining five sections are exactly the decisions this section

23
00:01:15,518 --> 00:01:19,954
walked you through, now written down in the order an engineer building against this

24
00:01:19,954 --> 00:01:21,771
document would actually want them.

25
00:01:21,771 --> 00:01:23,802
Notice the order itself is deliberate.

26
00:01:23,802 --> 00:01:27,222
Why doesn't it match the order this section taught the ideas in?

27
00:01:27,222 --> 00:01:31,230
Pipeline stages come before chunking, because you can't decide how to chunk

28
00:01:31,230 --> 00:01:35,559
something until you know whether re-ranking or query rewriting are even in scope.

29
00:01:35,559 --> 00:01:39,888
Retrieval targets come before failure coverage, because you need a number to know

30
00:01:39,888 --> 00:01:42,935
what counts as a failure worth naming in the first place.

31
00:01:42,935 --> 00:01:46,996
Sequence the document for the reader building from it, not for the order you

32
00:01:46,996 --> 00:01:48,119
happened to learn it.

33
00:01:48,669 --> 00:01:53,395
Recognise this move from Section three: the same rewrite, aimed at a different

34
00:01:53,395 --> 00:01:53,940
document.

35
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:56,485
The first sentence describes an intention.

36
00:01:56,485 --> 00:02:00,909
The second describes something a QA engineer could actually go and check.

37
00:02:00,909 --> 00:02:05,756
Notice every clause in that second sentence traces back to a specific lecture in

38
00:02:05,756 --> 00:02:06,544
this section.

39
00:02:06,544 --> 00:02:08,846
The scope is 5.2's ingestion decision.

40
00:02:08,846 --> 00:02:10,301
Per content type is 5.3.

41
00:02:10,301 --> 00:02:11,512
The numbers are 5.4.

42
00:02:11,512 --> 00:02:14,421
Live permissions and a re-index cadence are 5.6.

43
00:02:14,421 --> 00:02:16,905
The fallback is 5.5's silent-failure fix.

44
00:02:16,905 --> 00:02:18,723
None of it is new information.

45
00:02:18,723 --> 00:02:22,722
It's this section's six decisions, in one sentence instead of six.

46
00:02:22,722 --> 00:02:26,782
Try the same disagreement test from Section three on both versions.

47
00:02:26,782 --> 00:02:31,690
Nobody can push back meaningfully on "we'll use RAG to answer questions," because

48
00:02:31,690 --> 00:02:35,023
there's nothing specific enough in it to disagree with.

49
00:02:35,023 --> 00:02:40,052
Every clause in the second sentence is a place a reviewer could reasonably say that

50
00:02:40,052 --> 00:02:42,839
number's too low, or that cadence is too slow.

51
00:02:42,839 --> 00:02:47,323
A document built to survive that kind of pushback is doing its actual job.

52
00:02:47,873 --> 00:02:52,386
Say the honest caveat, because it's easy to skip the first row of that table and

53
00:02:52,386 --> 00:02:56,954
jump straight to designing pipelines, because pipelines are the interesting part.

54
00:02:56,954 --> 00:03:01,692
Remember the tree from Section two: prompt first, retrieval only once a missing fact

55
00:03:01,692 --> 00:03:02,933
genuinely requires it.

56
00:03:02,933 --> 00:03:07,276
A RAG design doc that never justifies why a better prompt or a simpler answer

57
00:03:07,276 --> 00:03:10,942
wouldn't have done is a document that assumed its own conclusion.

58
00:03:10,942 --> 00:03:15,116
Make the first section argue for RAG specifically, not assume it, the same

59
00:03:15,116 --> 00:03:18,106
discipline as naming a threshold before defending it.

60
00:03:18,106 --> 00:03:22,505
This matters more than it sounds like it should, because a retrieval system is

61
00:03:22,505 --> 00:03:24,874
genuinely expensive to build and maintain.

62
00:03:24,874 --> 00:03:29,555
It's an index to keep current, a permission model to enforce, chunking decisions to

63
00:03:29,555 --> 00:03:33,560
revisit, and a whole section's worth of failure modes to guard against.

64
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:38,016
Committing to all of that for a problem a better prompt would have solved in an

65
00:03:38,016 --> 00:03:42,133
afternoon is exactly the kind of cost this section keeps warning against.

66
00:03:42,683 --> 00:03:47,157
That's today's instruction, not a suggestion for later: write the justification

67
00:03:47,157 --> 00:03:49,593
before you write anything downstream of it.

68
00:03:49,593 --> 00:03:54,237
It should feel slightly uncomfortable, the way the acceptance criteria section did

69
00:03:54,237 --> 00:03:55,200
in Section three.

70
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:59,844
If you can't finish that first section convincingly, that's not a writing problem.

71
00:03:59,844 --> 00:04:04,374
It's the document correctly telling you this feature might not need retrieval at

72
00:04:04,374 --> 00:04:04,601
all.

73
00:04:04,601 --> 00:04:09,132
That outcome is a genuine success for this exercise, not a failed attempt at it.

74
00:04:09,132 --> 00:04:13,889
A design doc that talks you out of building a retrieval system you didn't need saved

75
00:04:13,889 --> 00:04:15,135
real engineering time.

76
00:04:15,135 --> 00:04:19,553
That's worth more than five neatly filled sections defending a decision nobody

77
00:04:19,553 --> 00:04:20,459
actually tested.

78
00:04:21,009 --> 00:04:22,593
One thing before you move on.

79
00:04:22,593 --> 00:04:25,215
Download the template, attached to this lecture.

80
00:04:25,215 --> 00:04:29,804
All six sections are in place, with the same prompts this lecture walked through, so

81
00:04:29,804 --> 00:04:32,808
you're filling gaps in, not starting from a blank page.

82
00:04:32,808 --> 00:04:36,850
Write that first section for real, for a feature you're actually close to.

83
00:04:36,850 --> 00:04:41,439
If the honest answer is "maybe this didn't need RAG," that's a more valuable finding

84
00:04:41,439 --> 00:04:45,099
today than five more sections built on a premise that doesn't hold.

85
00:04:45,099 --> 00:04:49,360
Next lecture is the workshop: the full design doc, filled in end to end, for a

86
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:52,747
support use case you'll recognise from earlier in this course.
